<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Own Hub privacy manifest.

	  WHAT THIS BINARY DOES TODAY. `Firebase.initializeApp` runs on every launch
	  (`lib/app_state/firebase_boot.dart`), and pubspec carries five Firebase
	  packages: firebase_core, firebase_auth, cloud_firestore, firebase_storage
	  and cloud_functions. Firebase mints an installation identifier and sends it
	  with every call it makes, so the app collects a device identifier whether
	  or not a woman ever signs in. Apple's category for that is DeviceID, and it
	  is declared below.

	  Not linked, and not tracking. `Feature.accountRequired` is off in this
	  build, so there is no sign-up, no account and nothing to link the
	  identifier to; there are no ad SDKs, no analytics SDK and no third party it
	  is shared with. NSPrivacyTracking stays false.

	  NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes is empty for the APP TARGET only. Every
	  required-reason API this product touches is touched by a package that ships
	  its own manifest, which Xcode merges into the privacy report at build time:
	  shared_preferences declares NSUserDefaults CA92.1, and the Google and MLKit
	  pods declare disk space, file timestamp, boot time and user defaults. If
	  the app target ever reads one of those APIs directly, it belongs here.

	  THE RELEASE THAT FLIPS `accountRequired` MUST ADD, before submission:
	    - NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePhoneNumber   (sign-in, LINKED, not tracking)
	    - NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID        (the Firebase uid, LINKED)
	    - NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeHealthFitness (workouts, meals and My cycle,
	      LINKED. Apple treats menstrual data as sensitive health data, so this
	      one is not optional and the App Store Connect answers must match)
	    - and, for as long as identity verification is live, the photographs the
	      camera permission string already describes: both sides of a national ID
	      card and one selfie. Apple's categories are
	      NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent for the images and
	      NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDataTypes for the government ID, both
	      LINKED, purpose AppFunctionality. They are deleted after the check, and
	      "deleted afterwards" is not an exemption from declaring them.
	    - NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeCrashData only if Crashlytics ever ships. It
	      is not in pubspec today.

	  This file, the App Privacy answers in App Store Connect and
	  docs/release/privacy-policy.md must say the same thing. A mismatch between
	  the three is one of the most common rejections. Verify with Xcode:
	  Product > Archive > Organizer > Generate Privacy Report.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
